{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgffMyicCkiY4JdUBzeMz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For high-spin octahedral d⁷ (ground term ⁴T₁g(F)), the fit gives **Dq = 1070 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 863 cm⁻¹**. The bands are assigned as ν₁ = 9 460 (⁴T₂g), ν₂ = 20 160 (⁴A₂g) and ν₃ = 21 160 cm⁻¹ (⁴T₁g(P)).\n\n**Ligand-field energies**\n\nTake the ⁴F term as zero, so ⁴P lies at 15B.\n- ⁴T₂g = +2Dq\n- ⁴A₂g = +12Dq\n- The two ⁴T₁g states mix. Their diagonal energies are –6Dq and 15B, and the off-diagonal term is 4Dq. This gives:\n - E± = ½(15B – 6Dq) ± ½√(225B² + 180BDq + 100Dq²)\n\n**Transition energies from the ground state E₋**\n- ν₁ = 2Dq – E₋\n- ν₂ = 12Dq – E₋\n- ν₃ = E₊ – E₋ = √(225B² + 180BDq + 100Dq²)\n\nTwo useful relations follow:\n- ν₂ – ν₁ = 10Dq\n- ν₂ + ν₃ – 3ν₁ = 15B\n\n**Choosing the assignment**\n\n1. If ν₂ = 20 160 cm⁻¹:\n - 10Dq = 20 160 – 9 460 = 10 700, so Dq = 1 070 cm⁻¹.\n - 15B = 20 160 + 21 160 – 28 380 = 12 940, so B = 862.7 cm⁻¹.\n - Check: these values predict ν₃ = √(448.1×10⁶) ≈ 21 170 cm⁻¹, compared with 21 160 observed. This is consistent.\n2. If instead ν₂ = 21 160 cm⁻¹:\n - Dq = 1 170 cm⁻¹ and B = 862.7 cm⁻¹.\n - These predict ν₃ ≈ 22 050 cm⁻¹, compared with 20 160 observed. This is inconsistent, so this assignment is rejected.\n\n**Result**\n- Dq = 1 070 cm⁻¹ (10Dq = 10 700 cm⁻¹)\n- B ≈ 863 cm⁻¹\n- ν₂/ν₁ ≈ 2.13, which is typical of high-spin Co(II).\n\nC cannot be found from these data. All three bands are spin-allowed quartet–quartet transitions, and their energies depend only on Dq and B.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1070, \"B\": 863, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"High-spin d7 fit gives Dq=1070 and B=863 consistently; C cannot be found from spin-allowed bands alone.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1659,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_token